      {/* Round-4 brief §5 (D350, 2026-11-15): the prose moved BELOW the answer and the controls, and this is
          the whole of that ruling's effect on reading order. The brief's acceptance is a stranger on a phone
          getting "when / where / how do I sign up" without scrolling, and the instrument is
          `node design/fold-answers.mjs` - at 390x844 the when-line and the venue line sat at y=822 and y=874
          against an 844 fold, with the page's two controls past it, so the answers were present but not on the
          first screen. Putting the computed meeting and the controls ahead of the two lede paragraphs lands all
          five answers above the fold at 390; no class in this block changed, so what moved is order, not type,
          spacing or colour (the fence that forbids the latter three). The lede's paragraphs stay the only prose
          below the controls, and `check:copy` and `check:content` read words, not positions, so they see the same
          page they read before this move — and before R9's hoist, which moved the lede's first sentence above
          this block rather than writing one. */}
      {/* Round-6 review, "what to do this week" item 1 and the Home/`Remove` row on the cost paragraph
          (D381): the two questions a stranger asks before deciding — what does it cost, do I have to belong —
          are answered here in the two sentences `src/content.json` carries, in the order the review ranked
          them. Both are operator-granted draft claims, and the ledger in
          `docs/reply-to-the-communication-review-round-6-2026-11-16.md` names them and their correction path.
          The paragraph this replaces — "Nothing here says what a meeting costs, and no price is printed."
          — is deleted, not reworded; the review's own verdict was that a sentence pointing at an unresolved
          cost converts an unasked question into an anxious one. */}
      {answerLines.length > 0 && (
        <p class='mt-3 text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body'>{answerLines.join(' ')}</p>
      )}

      <div>
        <div class='mt-6 sm:mt-8 sm:flex sm:justify-center lg:justify-start'>
          <div>
            {/* Round-6 review, Home/Revise and "this week" item 3 (D384): one click to conversion instead of
                three. The destination is `signUpPaths()`'s answer for the date the hero prints — the same call
                `EventRow.astro` makes for that row — and `scripts/check-rsvp-page.mjs` compares the painted
                bytes with that call, so the button cannot drift from the row. The label names the date, so the
                control says the outcome rather than the group (the review's note that "Join Our Group" is the
                only Title Case string on the site is true, and the fix is to stop capitalising a verb phrase).
                When no sign-up page exists for a printed date, `signUpFor` is null and the control falls back
                to `/join`, the club's own route (S033, D303), with a label that says what it is. */}
            <a
              href={signUpFor ? signUpFor.href : '/join'}
              target={signUpFor ? '_blank' : undefined}
              rel={signUpFor ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
              data-cta={signUpFor?.cta ?? 'join-page'}
              data-control="primary"
              data-rsvp-date={nextMeeting?.date ?? undefined}
              class='w-full flex items-center justify-center px-8 py-4 text-label font-semibold rounded-control text-band-text bg-ink hover:bg-accent-strong focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent transition'
              >{heroLabel}{signUpFor && <span class="sr-only">{newTabQualifier(heroLabel, signUpFor.platform)}</span>}</a
            >
          </div>
          {/* The secondary used to be `target=_blank` at Meetup's own events page, which made the site's own
              schedule page a dead end one click away from a third-party page. `/schedule` is an emitted route
              now (`dist/client/schedule/index.html`), so the label stays honest and `check:content`'s dead-path
              rule can see the target; Meetup stays one step further on, where the sign-up actually is.
              No `target`: an internal navigation that a visitor can go back from.
              2026-11-16 (D373): that route is now `/dates` — `dist/client/dates/index.html` — so the href below
              and the words the visitor reads name one page. The path in this note above is the artifact as it was
              measured on 2026-11-14. */}
          <div class='mt-3 sm:mt-0 sm:ml-3'>
            <a
              href="/dates"
              data-cta="dates-page"
              data-control="secondary"
              class='w-full flex items-center justify-center px-8 py-4 text-label font-semibold rounded-control text-ink bg-paper inset-shadow-rule hover:bg-tint focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent transition'
            >
              All dates
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      </div>

      {/* R9 (D357, 2026-11-16): this paragraph used to open with the sentence now sitting above the meeting
          block; what is left here is the paragraph's second sentence, word for word.
          Round-8 review (2026-11-18), Home copy line on line 19: "delete the first clause of line 19; make it:
          'Most of the arguing happens in the chat between meetings.'" The clause it deletes named the platform
          the band below already names, and the site's own fuller statement of that fact — "Dates and places go
          up on Meetup, and every date we list links to the page the club posts its sign-up on" — is kept in the
            threads band (the letter's Keep row on that sentence, and the defect D350 closed by keeping it).
          **The sentence that replacement produced is gone (S082, D454, 2026-11-19).** The operator on it: "we
          don't argue or debate, it's more of a collaborate environment of exploration" — a paragraph about what
          the club does, framed as a dispute, is copy this tree cannot source: it arrived from a seat's review
          letter (`docs/communication-review-round-8-2026-11-18.md`, Home copy line 19) and never went through a
          slot in `src/content.json`. Census row **H1b** flips to `want: 'absent'` in the same commit, so the
          instrument that demanded the sentence now refuses its return. **The sentence below is not clean either,
          and a future round must not assume it is.** "There is no syllabus, no reading list to keep up with, and no
          current focus." enters at `0f15df8` (2026-11-02) and "Nobody sets an agenda. People bring what they are
          curious about…" at `9a7c111` (2026-11-18, MS37's close) — seat copy typed into this component, with **no slot
          in `src/content.json` behind it**: `node design/frame-words.mjs` prints its "curious" as the only frame word
          in the artifact with no slot at all. It is also the only sentence any artifact uses to decline q06 ("what is
          the club's current focus?"), so removing it is a reader-facing act, not a cleanup — `git log -S` on this file
          and `node design/visitor-questions.mjs` are the two probes that price it. */}
      <p
        class='mt-3 text-lede leading-prose text-ink-2 sm:mt-6 font-body'
      >
        There is no syllabus, no reading list to keep up with, and no current focus. Nobody sets an agenda.
